Subject: pv cursor: use the shared lock file only if it is a private regular file

Why: with -c (cursor positioning), when the terminal itself cannot be locked, pv opens
$TMPDIR/pv-<tty>-<euid>.lock with O_RDWR|O_CREAT|O_NOFOLLOW and locks it with fcntl
(src/pv/cursor.c:101-125, 160-166). The file is shared on purpose by every pv of that euid on
that tty, so O_EXCL is not possible. On Android the wrapper's TMPDIR is /data/local/tmp
(shell shell 0771), where the shell user can create that name first: root's pv then locks the
shell user's file, which the shell user can hold locked to block it, and removes the name at
exit (:767-768). After the open, fstat the descriptor and use it only if it is a regular file
owned by the effective uid with at most one link (the same check nstat/ifstat of iproute2 make on their
history files, misc/nstat.c:685-687); otherwise close it and turn cursor positioning off, as
pv already does when the open fails.
Source: written for this module (see ndk/REFERENCES.md, pv).

--- a/src/pv/cursor.c
+++ b/src/pv/cursor.c
@@ -136,6 +136,28 @@
 		cursor->disable = true;
 		return;
 	}
+
+	/*
+	 * The lock file is shared on purpose (every pv of this euid on this
+	 * tty locks it), so it cannot be created with O_EXCL. In a directory
+	 * other users can write, such as Android's /data/local/tmp, another
+	 * user could create it first and hold the lock, or link it to a file
+	 * of this euid. Use it only if it is a regular file of this euid with
+	 * at most one link (another pv may already have removed the name at
+	 * exit), as nstat/ifstat check their history files; otherwise
+	 * turn cursor positioning off.
+	 */
+	{
+		struct stat sb;
+		if (fstat(cursor->lock_fd, &sb) != 0 || !S_ISREG(sb.st_mode) || sb.st_uid != geteuid()
+		    || sb.st_nlink > 1) {
+			pv_error("%s: %s", cursor->lock_file, _("lock file is not a private regular file"));
+			(void) close(cursor->lock_fd);
+			cursor->lock_fd = -1;
+			cursor->disable = true;
+			return;
+		}
+	}
 }
 
 
